VT_TALK("sql/sql-plans", {
 "explain": "SQL says what you want, not how to get it. A part of the engine called the query optimizer decides how, choosing which index to use, which table to read first and whether to sort. EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN shows that choice without running the query. Each line names a step. SCAN means read every row, SEARCH means jump in through an index, and USE TEMP B TREE means sort the result. In the animation, type a query or pick one, switch indexes on and off, and read the real plan SQLite chooses. PostgreSQL and MySQL have EXPLAIN and EXPLAIN ANALYZE, which also shows real timings. Think of a taxi app that plans the route before the driver sets off.",
 "joins": "A join is a question, and the engine has several ways to answer it. A nested loop join takes each row of one table and looks up matches in the other, which is fast when the inner table has an index on the join column. A hash join builds a hash table from the smaller input and probes it with the other, which suits big unsorted tables. A merge join sorts both sides and walks them together. SQLite uses nested loops and can build a temporary index when none exists, which is a hint that you should create one. The optimizer also chooses which table drives the loop. Think of matching socks, by picking one and searching the pile, or by sorting both piles first.",
 "stats": "Most optimizers are cost based. For each possible plan they estimate how many rows every step will return, add up a cost, and pick the cheapest. The idea comes from IBM's System R research in nineteen seventy nine. Those estimates rely on statistics about the data, such as how many rows a table has and how many rows share each value. ANALYZE gathers these statistics. If they go stale, for example after a big import, the optimizer can choose a plan that was right for a small table and terrible for a large one. Think of a delivery company planning routes with last year's traffic map.",
 "tuning": "Many slow queries are fixed with small rewrites. A condition the engine can answer through an index is called sargable. Wrapping the column in a function, such as taking the first seven characters of a date, hides it from the index, while a plain date range lets the engine search. Name only the columns you need. For paging, OFFSET still walks past every skipped row, so on deep pages remember the last id and ask for ids after it. And watch for the N plus one problem, where an app runs one query per item in a loop instead of one join for all of them. Think of buying all your groceries in one trip instead of one trip per item.",
 "levels": "Here is the ladder. For a beginner, the database decides how to run your query, and EXPLAIN shows the plan. At college level, read plans for scans and sorts, know the three join algorithms, keep statistics fresh and write sargable conditions. At masters level, optimizers search join orders with dynamic programming, and errors in row estimates grow with every join. At research level, people build optimizers that learn from past queries and adapt plans while they run. Think of a sat nav that learns which roads are busy at which hour."
});
